=== Episode A0 | Introduce Economics

Cold open.

=== Introduce Myself

I'm Professor Taylor Weidman, PhD. You can call me Taylor. Even though I can run a 5 minute mile, don't call me Taylor Swift.

I've been in the Econ department at Pitt for 10 years. And I've been teaching microeconomics for nearly as long.

I was born in Arlington VA. Anyone from the DMV?

I grew up in Richmond. Anyone from Richmond?

We then moved to south central PA. Anyone from that area?

I did undergrad at a small school in the Shenandoah Valley, did a masters in economics at Iowa State, then moved here for the PhD in 2016.

Every semester when choosing classes to teach, I jockey for this one. It's a real honor and privilege to walk through the ideas and remember why I fell in love with this field in a room full of bright minds. In this room I’m going to challenge you and you’re going to leave with a much deeper understanding of the way the world works.

=== Class Logistics

Time in this room will not be all lectures. Class time will be focussed on introducing ideas, giving you a structured time to practice, and developing the skills required to understand the ideas. There will be a mixture of animations, simulations, whiteboard work, and group work.

You have to take notes, writing down what you think is most important. The animations from class are posted online as we go, so you don't need to copy down what's on the screen. Your notes are for the thinking, not the transcription.

We have two lectures per week and then I'll send you off to your recitation. Recitations are equivalent to a lab. Recitations extend the work we do in this room, and help you prepare for Checkpoints. Your recitation instructors can be your first person of contact and Recitations are often a great place to meet other students.~

You'll get set up with Gradescope in your recitation this week.

The textbook for this class is completely free. It's OpenStax's Principles of Microeconomics, free to read online, with a copy posted on the course page. You don't need to buy anything for this class.

=== Talk to Me

When I was a student, and even to some degree in grad school, I was often intimidated or nervous to talk to my professors. You don't have to be that way with me. I'm an expert in this field but I want you to come talk to me. I regularly have students in my office. Because you don't know me yet, let me read some things my students have said in the past.

- Taylor is willing to help and explain topics.
- Taylor is very interested in the course and wants his students to succeed.
- Taylor understands his students and makes learning non-stressful.
- Taylor is very approachable and makes class interesting.

Come talk to me after class or in office hours or just say hi if you see me around campus! It's one of my favorite parts of my job.

=== Simulations

Economics is powerful and very human. In this classroom we'll often run little simulations to demonstrate economic principles. We're going to run our first one right now. Grading takes a lot of work. Who would be willing to skip every assignment in exchange for a guaranteed D-? Few if any. What about a guaranteed A? Everyone.

This is our first principle of microeconomics: People respond to incentives. This is the principle behind grades in this class.

=== Grades

Grading isn’t fun for anyone. I don’t like giving bad grades and you don’t like getting them. I expect you to be here every class barring emergencies, and one “I need a day off” day. If you’re stressing about the grade you’re going to get in the class, you’re almost certainly doing this class wrong.

I'm giving you a little gift. Your final grade is the percentage of assessed skills that you pass on Checkpoints and Reattempts. Checkpoints and Reattempts allow you to save your progress in the class: skills you pass stay passed. If you no-pass a skill on an in-class Checkpoint, you can try again during a Reattempt. Reattempts can only help you. If you pass 94% of the skills, your final grade is a 94%. There will be one Checkpoint for each of the six parts of the class.

Each skill has three types of practice: Exercises done together in class, Vignettes done together in recitation, and Homework done on your own time. Complete 2 of 3 types of practice for a skill to unlock a first attempt, and 3 of 3 to unlock a reattempt. Practice is graded for completion not correctness. You're allowed to make mistakes.

You try something on the Homework or in Recitation, you do it wrong, and you then learn how to do it right. That's the learning process. Put in the reps before the Checkpoints and show me your best work on the Checkpoints.

Practice cannot be made up before a Checkpoint. But you may make up any of your missed practice after the Checkpoint during the next Reattempt.

I don't grade for a distribution. This means that you're not competing against your classmates for an A. In fact, I ask that you help each other out. Everyone can get an A and I'd be thrilled. All you need is to pass the standard.

I want you to feel pressure to put in the work, to dig into the material, to go the extra mile. But I don’t want you to feel that pressure from me. I want you to buy into the structure, to hand the assignment to yourself, and then ask me for feedback by turning it in. If you’re struggling I need you to promise you’ll reach out to me.

I think grades can be valuable if we think about them properly, not as a measure of how gifted or valuable one is, but like markings on the road, helping to tell us which direction to go.

A grade tells me how much you can think critically about microeconomic ideas, but it tells me very little about you as a person. You’ll receive the same level of respect from me whether you’re getting an A or D- in the class.

=== Peer Mentors

You’re STRONGLY encouraged to work in groups on Homework and studying. I’d go as far as to say that if you’re not working on homework in a group, you’re not doing this class right. This is so true, that I want you to get the names of three others in the class and add them to the top of the first page of your notebook from today.

These are your microeconomics peer mentors. You don’t need to be in touch with them or even be friends. But by putting your name down in someone’s notebook, you’re giving them the authorization to contact you about the class at any point, next week, next month, or right before finals.

I hope you see that I put a lot of thought and energy to give my students all the resources to succeed. In return I ask a few things:

- First, take care of those around you. This is a big room. Make sure if those around you are struggling to help out. How many of you have taken AP economics? You've likely seen some of what we're doing here and you might be of some help to others.
- Second, if you're lost in the class or at Pitt, you’re not alone. I ask that you reach out to someone. That's a difficult thing to do but it's something I ask of you.
- Third I ask that you show up with your most authentic and committed self to put in the reps. You'll get the reps in this room, recitation, homework, and demos. I ask that you do it all.

My goal is to show you why these ideas have been some of the most influential in modern history and walk you through the skills required to understand them.

