Last time we showed that countries could open their domestic borders to international trade and do better. This was the origical framing of the Molly and Andrew, spinach and carrots story we told at the beginning of the semester. We were able to show that no matter whether the global price was above the domestic equilibrium or below the domstic equilibrium, we could increase total surplus, the sum of consumer surplus, through either imports or exports.

Today we're going to continue with this story by complicating the picture a bit. We teach this every semester. It's a bit of a political hot topic at the moment. In a bit we'll use the model to show why this topic is politically complicated. In this classroom we're not going to spend any time discussing the politics of the policies but simply consider the model and how it relates to the world.

Last time we told a story of the steel industry. The bessimer process was a new technology that started to expand globally after WWII, leading to a global shift out in the supply curve.

Lets say we have two countries, the US and China. Lets say China's domestic price is below the global price. Let's start by thinking about the two countries separately and what would happen to them if we openned the domestic markets up for international trade.

Lets start by thinking about what the Chineese impacts might be.

If China opens it's borders to international trade, since the global price is above the domestic price, the domestic price will rise as domestic sellers can now get a better deal selling internationally. This benefits Chinese steel producers. The price goes for those already in the market and more people get to enter the market, which leads to more jobs in the steel industry. This is great for you. On the buyer's side, prices go up because domestic sellers aren't willing to buy from you.

On the other side of the pacific, if the US opens it's borders up to trade ...

We've implemented these kinds of policies in recent history. Have you heard of NAFTA? Or the Trans-Pacific Parternship? These are some examples of trade deals the US has been involved with. NAFTA was a trade deal between north american countries, US, Canada, and Mexico. The idea is exactly as we've layed out here, except we would replace China with Mexico in the model.

NAFTA was developed specifically to capture the increase in total surplus that would be generated by specialization and trade. Mexico had lower prices for some goods, which would lead to exports, while the US had higher prices for those goods, which would lead to imports. We've shown that this type of thing is total surplus improving. This is what policymakers knew and were trying to achieve.

But we've also said that the benefits of trade deals like this aren't equally shared. You'll see that in the US, even though total surplus went up, primarily through the increase in Consumer Surplus, Producer Surplus took a real hit. We knew this would happen, that we would be transfering total surplus from sellers to buyers. Instead of letting this play itself out, buyers getting a benefit while sellers taking a hit, we knew the deal on its own, to improve surplus would also transfer surplus, we could have given a bonus to sellers. If you worked in a steel mill that got shut down due to imports, you would be compensated.

But we never did it. It was obvious even at the time. This led to political pushback against trade deals that we still feel today. You many have heard of the Trans-Pacific Partnership. This would have been a trade deal between many countries on the pacific. But US political backlash to trade deals like NAFTA

